<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chaghaniyan</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Chaghaniyan was a medieval principality in the valley of the Surkhan Darya, in present-day southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbekistan">Uzbekistan</a>, north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxus">Amu Darya</a>. Ruled by local princes titled Chaghan Khudah, it was one of the lordships of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokharistan">Tokharistan</a> under the Western Turks before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/early-muslim-conquests">Arab conquests</a> of the 8th century. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samanid-empire">Samanids</a> its Muhtajid dynasty remained powerful governors.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chaghaniyan.jpg" alt="Chaghaniyan" width="320" height="695" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Painting: Unknown 7th century artist.Photographer: undetermined · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Delegate_from_Chaganian_in_the_Afrasiab_murals_650_CE.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
